#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc723e84-3051-5e73-ac57-08da7193c368" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Squares Under the Radical"}] :question/id #uuid "dd7c79e5-d274-5f04-af44-335b5a6d95df" :question/math-academy-id "e-10699" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4z^2}{9}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac {4z^2} {9}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{4z^2}} {\\sqrt{9}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{\\sqrt{3^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2z|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2|\\cdot |z|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot |z|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2|z|}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f03bcc0e-23e1-534b-81bb-146079d37c3e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Radical Expression With Domain Restrictions"}] :question/id #uuid "40af3166-710b-508c-9a8e-2c54365cd238" :question/math-academy-id "e-10712" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}}$ given that $x > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}} = \\sqrt {\\dfrac {32x^5} {2x^3}} = \\sqrt {16x^2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt {16x^2}&= \\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt {4^2x^2}&= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sqrt {(4x)^2}&= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t|4x| &=\\[5pt]\n|4| \\cdot |x| &=\\[5pt]\n4 \\cdot |x|\n\t}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n4 \\cdot |x| &=\\[5pt]\n4 \\cdot x &=\\[5pt]\n4x.\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068444 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eeca0971-59ed-576d-a248-293decd655b1" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Fourths Under the Radical"}] :question/id #uuid "9ceac090-9a1c-57d7-a311-f771ece6e0b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10716" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac {45a^4} {5} }= \\sqrt{9a^4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |3{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot {a}^{2} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc723e84-3051-5e73-ac57-08da7193c368" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Squares Under the Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062d7dd5-f242-5a3b-9405-9a5b5ea96bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7d}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502993} {:answer/id #uuid "2a275a66-ebf4-5205-a5ef-cde8f8dbe9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503061} {:answer/id #uuid "b37dac13-b301-58de-a731-8916f547f6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4|d|}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503348} {:answer/id #uuid "e846052d-00aa-51a2-bb65-4538a6247a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503459} {:answer/id #uuid "f563e148-82b5-5280-9458-176982ebb37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4|d|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b37dac13-b301-58de-a731-8916f547f6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4|d|}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503348} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae423bd2-3a38-5bbb-a890-bc1ea84e6baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1335ac60-8bcf-5956-af28-bb3838c1f5f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162907" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{16{d}^{2}}{49}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{16{d}^{2}}{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4d)^{2}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4d|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |d|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |d|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|d|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc723e84-3051-5e73-ac57-08da7193c368" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Squares Under the Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013e435b-60eb-5378-b398-aed373e0a7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2|k|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502981} {:answer/id #uuid "22250168-100e-5b9e-8fd7-96f4a7947d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4|k|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503047} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7ed17d-d022-575f-bb63-efbbf8963701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18|k|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503333} {:answer/id #uuid "dd284de2-fe3b-5cd8-b881-4715aed76717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4k}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503428} {:answer/id #uuid "fa620601-fae5-51be-8d5c-fcf623fc1b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22250168-100e-5b9e-8fd7-96f4a7947d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4|k|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503047} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f9ae805-7144-5790-b998-04aad918bbdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31429a2f-5c61-530a-8222-8a7650300604" :question/math-academy-id "q-162911" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{16{k}^{2}}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{16{k}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{81}}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{9}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}}=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{(4k)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|4k|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|4|\\cdot |k|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4\\cdot |k|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4|k|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eeca0971-59ed-576d-a248-293decd655b1" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Fourths Under the Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4a83e0-737b-5b67-b64b-200fc255177b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7|z|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503099} {:answer/id #uuid "5da55407-8883-53ea-b5c6-491837548baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503163} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa63f28-de50-5192-abd5-b8c2bb8902a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503364} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c73b4e-428e-50cc-93e0-cbd14e3b1e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{z}^{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503385} {:answer/id #uuid "fa300cbc-65c1-5757-b142-52fb10a55301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2|z|}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9c73b4e-428e-50cc-93e0-cbd14e3b1e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{z}^{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503385} :answer-field/id #uuid "8026c441-d57b-5d0b-9cf5-6a390d5fe400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0691dce4-a0cf-573f-ba88-5eaf489529db" :question/math-academy-id "q-162926" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{4{z}^{4}}{49}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{4{z}^{4}}{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(2{z}^{2})^{2}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2{z}^{2}|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2|\\cdot |{z}^{2}|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot |{z}^{2}|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |{z}^{2}|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {z}^{2}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2{z}^{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068838 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eeca0971-59ed-576d-a248-293decd655b1" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Fourths Under the Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44fbf470-b73e-59c0-88a9-4c46a647db06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503113} {:answer/id #uuid "7077dca4-6231-5f02-a788-1ad28179ed34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503207} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2a658c-d5e3-56ad-a06c-c64d2889f443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{a}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503329} {:answer/id #uuid "cb239db5-410c-5e11-903a-4547890ad3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503390} {:answer/id #uuid "cc350026-beb3-51bc-9cae-86763e7a5745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{a}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad2a658c-d5e3-56ad-a06c-c64d2889f443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{a}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503329} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d51e237-68b4-5192-8980-07627460502d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53830319-4c61-59d6-b464-6d52b63f8b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162932" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{12{a}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{12{a}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{12{a}^{4}}{3}}=\\sqrt{4{a}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{a}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |2{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot {a}^{2} & = \\\\ 2{a}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f03bcc0e-23e1-534b-81bb-146079d37c3e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Radical Expression With Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00477674-38ab-5163-82a2-4d64cf1fc38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{11{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502979} {:answer/id #uuid "0f555709-6ad9-5d19-9d75-aedc3ea5e163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503017} {:answer/id #uuid "779d3383-19e0-50ea-9d73-d522be2f3d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{11t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503221} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5f45b0-c236-5acc-9c82-844150388863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503324} {:answer/id #uuid "b195dfc3-03fb-5220-941c-824823148fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{11t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "779d3383-19e0-50ea-9d73-d522be2f3d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{11t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503221} :answer-field/id #uuid "d917ef30-e7dc-5bfc-871f-18df149530e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2aa05db-1a86-5603-983d-94f822e3df23" :question/math-academy-id "q-162959" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{121{t}^{2}}}$ given that $t<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36}}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(11t)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{|11t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{|11|\\cdot |t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{11\\cdot |t|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $t<0,$ so we have $|t|=-t.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{11\\cdot |t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{11\\cdot (-t)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{6}{11t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f03bcc0e-23e1-534b-81bb-146079d37c3e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Radical Expression With Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c74308bb-5312-5958-b720-065684db080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066553} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} {:answer/id #uuid "28a7f235-57a0-53cc-a737-590d73e17205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066556} {:answer/id #uuid "23dbda11-5ccf-5052-90ff-4a04744a31b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503050} {:answer/id #uuid "d40037db-e219-58c5-9dc7-f0a266a679e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} :answer-field/id #uuid "46437d48-53c7-532c-8f90-902783817395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae074857-e82d-57ff-b966-0c2516135214" :question/math-academy-id "q-3094" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}$ given that $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{50x}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{5})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |\\frac{2x}{5}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|2x|}{|5|} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0,$ so we have $|x|=x.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |x|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot x}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{2x}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
